---
/*
  /blog — the Index (design.md §4): a retrieval room, machinery register.

  A content-type switch (Writing · Quotes) — the primary axis — sits above a
  sticky left rail (search · subject filters) and the scrolling feed. Writing
  renders as excerpt cards that open the full essay in an in-context Reader;
  quotes, being short, render whole. Everything is URL-driven — `?view`,
  `?subject`, `?q`, `?page` — so any state is shareable.

  ⚠ THE RAIL IS THE SAME FOR BOTH VIEWS BECAUSE BOTH ARE TEXT, and that has been
  the load-bearing sentence here through two rewrites. Quotes and writing are
  retrieval over a shared subject taxonomy, so they are the same KIND of room and
  share its machinery.

  ⚠ AND MUSIC LEFT ON 2026-08-21 (ADR 0040). It was a third tab here from
  2026-08-11 — `?view=music`, no rail, no feed, no search, no pagination,
  filtered by a different vocabulary — which is a room's worth of exceptions
  wearing a view's clothes. It is `/listening` now, and the `?view=music`
  redirect below is what remains of it. What is left is a two-view room whose two
  views genuinely take the same machinery, which is what this file was built for.

  Search mirrors the admin (docs/search.md): a debounced fetch-and-swap of
  #blog-feed (see scripts/blog-feed.ts) with match highlighting, gated at
  MIN_SEARCH on both server and client. That same script runs the infinite
  scroll — an IntersectionObserver that appends #feed-list pages via the
  #feed-sentinel's data-next-url.
*/
import SiteLayout from '../../layouts/SiteLayout.astro';
import Label from '../../components/Label.astro';
import PostCard from '../../components/PostCard.astro';
import QuoteCard from '../../components/QuoteCard.astro';
import Reader from '../../components/Reader.astro';
import QuoteArticle from '../../components/QuoteArticle.astro';
import { Icon } from 'astro-icon/components';
import { attachNeighbourhoods, listWriting, listQuotes, listSubjects, neighbourhoodsFor } from '../../lib/blog';

import { MIN_SEARCH } from '../../lib/search-highlight';

const supabase = Astro.locals.supabase;
const params = Astro.url.searchParams;
const raw = params.get('view');

/*
  ⚠ THE MUSIC ROOM'S OLD ADDRESS, AND IT IS A 301 RATHER THAN A DROP (ADR 0040).
  `?view=music` was a public, shareable URL for ten days: it is in browser
  histories, in two ADRs, and in whatever Michael sent anyone. `?set=` rides
  along, because the thing worth sharing about this room was always ONE set and
  losing the slug would land a sent link on somebody else's playlist.

  ⚠ HAND-BUILT `Response`, FOR THE REASON `constellations.astro` GIVES AT
  LENGTH: a page that RETURNS a Response short-circuits the render, so
  `Astro.response.headers` never reaches the wire and `Astro.redirect()` builds
  the object out of reach. Uncached, every stale link costs a function
  invocation to be told something that is true forever. Same pair of numbers as
  that file, and for the same asymmetry — the edge can be purged on a deploy and
  a stranger's disk cannot, so the promise made to the CDN is the bolder one.

  It sits above every read on this page on purpose: a request that is leaving
  should not pay for the feed, the rail, or the subject taxonomy first.

  ⚠ AND THE `?set=` IS READ TWICE RATHER THAN HELD IN A CONST, WHICH LOOKS LIKE
  SOMETHING TO TIDY AND IS NOT. `astro check` reports any local whose ONLY use
  is inside an early-returning frontmatter block as `ts(6133) declared but never
  read` — the generated TSX loses the reference. Three shapes were tried
  (`const set` inside the block, a derived `const to`, both hoisted above the
  `if`) and each produced a false hint on a line that is plainly read. Reading
  the param twice is the version that compiles clean and says something true.
*/
if (raw === 'music') {
  return new Response(null, {
    status: 301,
    headers: {
      Location: `/listening${params.get('set') ? `?set=${encodeURIComponent(params.get('set') ?? '')}` : ''}`,
      'Cache-Control': 'public, max-age=3600, s-maxage=31536000',
    },
  });
}

const view = raw === 'quotes' ? 'quotes' : 'writing';
const type = view === 'quotes' ? 'quote' : 'writing';

// Subjects STACK (AND). Comma-joined in the URL: ?subject=uncertainty,emotion.
const selectedSubjects = Array.from(
  new Set(
    (params.get('subject') ?? '')
      .split(',')
      .map((s) => s.trim())
      .filter(Boolean),
  ),
);
// One author, not a list: "everything by this person" is a single question, and
// stacking two authors would be an OR where every other filter here is an AND.
//
// QUOTES ONLY, and read as nothing anywhere else — only a quote has an author
// row. Left ungated, `/blog?author=seneca` on the writing view would draw the
// chip and head the feed "12 essays by Seneca" over an unfiltered list, since
// `listWriting` has no such facet to apply. A filter that is drawn but not
// applied is the same lie as a count that ignores one.
const authorSlug = view === 'quotes' ? (params.get('author') ?? '').trim() || null : null;
const rawQ = (params.get('q') ?? '').trim();
const searching = rawQ.length >= MIN_SEARCH; // a 1-char term matches ~everything (docs/search.md §3)
const q = searching ? rawQ : '';
// `page` is internal to infinite scroll: only client fetches (X-Requested-With)
// ask for a specific page; a real browser navigation always starts at page 1, so
// a stray ?page=N in the address bar can never show a partial feed.
const isPartial = Astro.request.headers.get('X-Requested-With') === 'fetch';
const page = isPartial ? Math.max(1, Number.parseInt(params.get('page') ?? '1', 10) || 1) : 1;

// The rail's subjects are annotated for the CURRENT filter: a contextual count,
// which are selected, and which combinations are dead-ends (disabled).
//
// ⚠ EVERY FILTER GOES IN, NOT JUST THE SUBJECTS. The search term re-counts the
// tags as you type, and the author does the same — arriving from an attribution
// narrows the corpus to one person, and a rail still counting all 77 quotes
// would offer thirteen tags that person never used. See `listSubjects`.
//
// Unconditional again since music left (ADR 0040): both remaining views have the
// rail, so there is no longer a view on this page for which these three queries
// would be spent on a control that is not drawn.
const rail = await listSubjects(supabase, type, { selected: selectedSubjects, q: q || null, author: authorSlug });
const subjects = rail.subjects;
const nameBySlug = new Map(subjects.map((s) => [s.slug, s.name]));
// slug → GLOBAL count, so the feed's per-card subjects sort busiest-first (they
// carry no count of their own) in an order that never shifts as you filter.
const subjectRank = new Map(subjects.map((s) => [s.slug, s.total]));

// The rail shows the whole taxonomy on desktop, but a long list crowds the top
// of the mobile page — so we cap it there and reveal the tail behind "Show all".
//
// ⚠ THE CAP IS BY POSITION, NOT BY LIVENESS, AND THAT WAS CHECKED RATHER THAN
// ASSUMED. Ordering is global (see `listSubjects`), so under a narrow filter the
// first eight can in principle all be dead ends — eight grey tags over "Show all
// (18)" reads as a broken rail. Measured against the corpus when the author
// filter learned to count (2026-08-11): of 35 authors, 3 would show an all-dead
// first eight, and all 3 have exactly one quote. A one-quote author is
// UNREACHABLE by the door that leads here — the attribution only offers "N more
// from …" when N > 0 — so the case needs a hand-typed URL to reach at all.
// Sorting live-first would fix a page nobody can arrive at, and would cost the
// stability rule on every page they can. Left alone on purpose; revisit if the
// taxonomy grows past ~30 or a one-quote author ever gets a door.
const SUBJECT_CAP = 8;
const subjectOverflow = Math.max(0, subjects.length - SUBJECT_CAP);

const writing =
  view === 'writing'
    ? await listWriting(supabase, { page, subjects: selectedSubjects, q: q || null, subjectRank })
    : null;
// ⚠ ONE BATCH FOR THE PAGE, not one per card. Each card carries a full essay in
// a reader template, and each of those closes with a strip that wants the same
// three questions answered — asked per essay that is twenty-eight round trips on
// the busiest route on the site.
if (writing) await attachNeighbourhoods(supabase, writing.items);

const quotes =
  view === 'quotes'
    ? await listQuotes(supabase, { page, subjects: selectedSubjects, q: q || null, author: authorSlug, subjectRank })
    : null;
/*
  The quotes view fills the Reader from a `<template>` per card, so each one
  needs its whole apparatus — and ONE batch answers all twenty-five, the same
  way the constellation suite does it. Asked per card this would be a hundred
  round trips on a view that currently makes two.
*/
const quoteSheets = quotes
  ? await neighbourhoodsFor(
      supabase,
      quotes.items.map((q) => ({
        id: q.id,
        quote: q,
        authorName: q.author?.name ?? null,
        authorSlug: q.author?.slug ?? null,
        // Already counted once for the cards' own doors — handing it over saves
        // asking the same question twice on one render.
        authorOthers: q.authorSiblings,
      })),
    )
  : new Map();

const feed = writing ?? quotes;

const noun = view === 'quotes' ? 'quote' : 'essay';
const nounPlural = view === 'quotes' ? 'quotes' : 'essays';
// The search field names the medium ("writing"/"quotes"), not the unit ("essays").
const searchNoun = view === 'quotes' ? 'quotes' : 'writing';

/** Build a /blog URL from the current view + the given subjects / query / page. */
function buildHref(
  over: { subjects?: string[]; q?: string | null; page?: number | null; author?: string | null } = {},
): string {
  const p = new URLSearchParams();
  if (view === 'quotes') p.set('view', 'quotes');
  const subs = over.subjects ?? selectedSubjects;
  if (subs.length) p.set('subject', subs.join(','));
  // Carried by default: the author is a filter like any other, so toggling a
  // subject or typing a search must NARROW within that person rather than
  // silently drop back to the whole corpus.
  const who = over.author !== undefined ? over.author : authorSlug;
  if (who) p.set('author', who);
  const query = over.q !== undefined ? over.q : q || null;
  if (query) p.set('q', query);
  if (over.page) p.set('page', String(over.page));
  const s = p.toString();
  return s ? `/blog?${s}` : '/blog';
}

/** The URL after toggling one subject in/out of the selection — the search term
 *  is kept, since subjects and search compose rather than reset each other. */
function toggleHref(slug: string): string {
  const next = selectedSubjects.includes(slug)
    ? selectedSubjects.filter((s) => s !== slug)
    : [...selectedSubjects, slug];
  return buildHref({ subjects: next, page: null });
}

/** Clears every filter (subjects + search + author) while staying on the view. */
const clearHref = buildHref({ subjects: [], q: null, page: null, author: null });

// The name to SHOW. Resolved from the corpus rather than from the slug, because
// "marcus-aurelius" un-slugified is a guess and this is a person's name. An
// unknown slug returns no quotes at all (listQuotes' rule), and the chip then
// names the slug so the URL is at least legible while it is being corrected.
const authorName = authorSlug
  ? ((await supabase.from('authors').select('name').eq('slug', authorSlug).maybeSingle()).data?.name ?? authorSlug)
  : null;

// ⚠ TWO TABS, AND THE SWITCH IS STILL THE MASTHEAD (ADR 0030). A third entry
// pointing at `/listening` was the tempting version of ADR 0040 and loses twice:
// it would put a chrome destination inside a control whose whole job is naming
// the views of THIS page, and it would say "where you are" a second time, two
// rows under the bar that just said it. The room left; its tab left with it.
const tabs = [
  { key: 'writing', label: 'Writing', href: '/blog', active: view === 'writing' },
  { key: 'quotes', label: 'Quotes', href: '/blog?view=quotes', active: view === 'quotes' },
];

// Infinite scroll: the next page's URL, or null at the end. The client fetches it
// (X-Requested-With) and appends the items; the manual link is the no-JS fallback.
const nextUrl = feed && feed.page < feed.pageCount ? buildHref({ page: feed.page + 1 }) : null;

/*
  ⚠ NOT WHEN A READ BEHIND THIS PAGE FAILED (2026-08-27). The Index degrades to
  "nothing here yet" — right for the one reader who arrives mid-outage, and
  ruinous at the edge, where `stale-while-revalidate` would serve an emptied
  blog for a day after a few seconds of trouble. That is the amplifier that
  turned one 401 into an empty front door; `/` and the other public rooms
  already branch, and this is the last of them.

  ⚠ THE RAIL COUNTS, NOT ONLY THE FEED. `listSubjects` reports its own failure
  because its worst case is not an empty rail: a failed SEARCH read leaves the
  term unapplied and renders corpus-wide counts under a search box, which its
  own header calls worse than having no facet at all. Wrong numbers are not
  cacheable either.
*/
const degraded = !!feed?.failed || rail.failed;
Astro.response.headers.set(
  'Cache-Control',
  degraded ? 'private, no-store' : 'public, s-maxage=60, stale-while-revalidate=86400',
);
// The rendered page depends on X-Requested-With (partial page fetches honour
// ?page; browser navigations always start at page 1), so cache it per-variant.
Astro.response.headers.set('Vary', 'X-Requested-With');
---

<SiteLayout
  title="Blog"
  active="blog"
  description="Essays and collected lines — the writing, and the words of other people worth keeping."
>
  <div class="mx-auto w-full max-w-6xl px-5 py-8 sm:py-10">
    {
      /* The page heading, for the document outline + screen readers. The visible
        anchor is the Writing/Quotes switch below, so this stays sr-only. */
    }
    <h1 class="sr-only">Blog — {view === 'quotes' ? 'Quotes' : 'Writing'}</h1>
    {/* ─────────  PRIMARY AXIS: content-type switch  ───────── */}
    <nav class="border-base-300 mb-8 flex flex-wrap items-baseline gap-x-7 gap-y-2 border-b" aria-label="Content">
      {
        tabs.map((t) => (
          <a
            href={t.href}
            aria-current={t.active ? 'page' : undefined}
            class:list={[
              'font-display -mb-px border-b-2 pb-3 text-2xl leading-none font-light transition-colors sm:text-3xl',
              t.active ? 'border-primary text-base-content' : 'text-whisper hover:text-muted border-transparent',
            ]}
          >
            {t.label}
          </a>
        ))
      }
      {
        /* ⚠ THE GREYED-OUT "Music soon" WORD STOOD HERE AND IS GONE WITH THE
          ROOM (ADR 0040). It was §9 made visible — a room is not announced
          until there is something behind it — and both halves of that sentence
          have now been answered elsewhere: eight published sets, announced in
          the top bar of every page on the site. */
      }
    </nav>

    {
      /* The guard is now only TypeScript's: `feed` is `writing ?? quotes` and
        exactly one of those is populated on either remaining view, but the
        compiler cannot see that through two ternaries. It used to carry a
        `view !== 'music'` beside it that was a real branch — the music room had
        no feed and no rail, and the subtree was NOT RENDERED rather than
        hidden, so a page that filtered by neither never shipped a search box or
        a subject taxonomy. That room is `/listening` now (ADR 0040). */
    }
    {
      feed && (
        <div class="grid gap-8 md:grid-cols-[13rem_1fr] md:gap-12">
          {/* ─────────  THE RAIL: search + subjects (the two TEXT views)  ───────── */}
          <aside class="md:sticky md:top-20 md:self-start">
            <form method="get" action="/blog" role="search" id="blog-filters" class="relative">
              <Icon
                name="ph:magnifying-glass"
                class="text-whisper pointer-events-none absolute top-1/2 left-3 -translate-y-1/2 text-base"
              />
              <input
                type="search"
                name="q"
                value={rawQ}
                placeholder={`Search ${searchNoun}…`}
                aria-label={`Search ${searchNoun}`}
                autocomplete="off"
                class="rounded-field border-base-300 bg-base-100 placeholder:text-whisper focus:border-base-content/30 w-full border py-2 pr-3 pl-9 font-sans text-sm focus:outline-none"
              />
              {/* No-JS fallback: a search submit carries the selected subjects along
              (comma-joined). With JS, the client reads the live URL instead. */}
              {selectedSubjects.length > 0 && <input type="hidden" name="subject" value={selectedSubjects.join(',')} />}
              {/* Same reason as `subject` above: without this, searching inside one
              author's lines silently widens to the whole corpus with no JS. */}
              {authorSlug && <input type="hidden" name="author" value={authorSlug} />}
              {view === 'quotes' && <input type="hidden" name="view" value="quotes" />}
            </form>

            {/*
              ⚠ THE AUTHOR FILTER GETS A CHIP, and an earlier version of this
              file argued it should not — *"the rail's Clear already drops every
              filter, and a second dismissal control would be the only ✕ on a
              page whose subjects, search and view all clear the same way."* That
              was wrong about where a reader looks. Michael: *"I think we still
              need to show the filter chip … right now there is nothing showing
              you in the filter section that that's what it's filtering by."*

              The summary above the feed does say "20 quotes by Seneca" — but the
              RAIL is the instrument panel, and an active filter that appears
              nowhere on it reads as no filter at all. Every other one is visible
              and individually removable here; this one arrives by a link from a
              quote's attribution, which is the path where a reader is least
              likely to know what just happened.

              ABOVE Subjects rather than among them, because it is a different
              KIND of filter — one author, never a stack — and mixing it into a
              list that stacks would promise it composes the same way. Same chip
              register, so it still reads as the same instrument.
            */}
            {authorName && (
              <div class="mt-7">
                <Label variant="chrome">Who said it</Label>
                <div class="mt-3">
                  <a
                    href={buildHref({ author: null, page: null })}
                    data-author-chip
                    aria-current="true"
                    class="border-base-content/30 bg-base-200 text-base-content md:rounded-field inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 rounded-full border px-2.5 py-0.5 font-sans text-xs transition-colors"
                  >
                    {authorName}
                    <Icon name="ph:x" class="text-whisper size-3" aria-hidden="true" />
                  </a>
                </div>
              </div>
            )}

            {subjects.length > 0 && (
              <div class="mt-7" data-subjects>
                <div class="flex items-baseline justify-between gap-2">
                  <Label variant="chrome">Subjects</Label>
                  {(selectedSubjects.length > 0 || q || authorName) && (
                    <a
                      href={clearHref}
                      data-subject-clear
                      class="text-whisper hover:text-muted inline-flex items-center gap-0.5 font-sans text-xs transition-colors"
                    >
                      Clear
                      <Icon name="ph:x" class="size-3" />
                    </a>
                  )}
                </div>
                {/* One list, three states: SELECTED (filled, ✕ to remove),
                  SELECTABLE (its count = what adding it would still yield), or a
                  DEAD-END (muted + inert: nothing combines it with the filter). */}
                <ul class="mt-3 flex flex-wrap gap-1.5 md:flex-col md:gap-0.5">
                  {subjects.map((s, i) => (
                    <li class:list={[i >= SUBJECT_CAP && 'subject-extra']}>
                      {s.disabled ? (
                        <span
                          aria-disabled="true"
                          title="Nothing here combines with your current filter"
                          class="border-base-300/60 text-base-content/25 md:rounded-field inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 rounded-full border px-2.5 py-0.5 font-sans text-xs"
                        >
                          {s.name}
                          <span class="text-base-content/20">{s.count}</span>
                        </span>
                      ) : (
                        <a
                          href={toggleHref(s.slug)}
                          data-subject-link
                          aria-current={s.selected ? 'true' : undefined}
                          class:list={[
                            'md:rounded-field inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 rounded-full border px-2.5 py-0.5 font-sans text-xs transition-colors',
                            s.selected
                              ? 'border-base-content/30 bg-base-200 text-base-content'
                              : 'border-base-300 text-whisper hover:border-base-content/25 hover:text-base-content',
                          ]}
                        >
                          {s.name}
                          {s.selected ? (
                            <Icon name="ph:x" class="text-whisper size-3" aria-hidden="true" />
                          ) : (
                            <span class="text-whisper">{s.count}</span>
                          )}
                        </a>
                      )}
                    </li>
                  ))}
                </ul>
                {subjectOverflow > 0 && (
                  <button
                    type="button"
                    data-subjects-toggle
                    data-label-collapsed={`Show all (${subjects.length})`}
                    aria-expanded="false"
                    class="text-whisper hover:text-base-content/80 mt-2 font-sans text-xs transition-colors md:hidden"
                  >
                    Show all ({subjects.length})
                  </button>
                )}
              </div>
            )}
          </aside>

          {/* ─────────  THE FEED  ───────── */}
          <section class="min-w-0">
            <div id="blog-feed">
              {(selectedSubjects.length > 0 || q || authorName) && (
                <p class="text-whisper mb-8 font-serif italic">
                  {feed.total} {feed.total === 1 ? noun : nounPlural}
                  {/* ⚠ "by <Name>" LEADS, before the subjects. Arriving here from a
                    quote's attribution, the author is the question you asked and
                    everything else is a narrowing of it — reading "12 quotes on
                    grief by Seneca" puts the answer second. */}
                  {authorName && (
                    <>
                      {' '}
                      by <span class="text-base-content/80 not-italic">{authorName}</span>
                    </>
                  )}
                  {selectedSubjects.length > 0 && (
                    <>
                      {' '}
                      on{' '}
                      <span class="text-base-content/80 not-italic">
                        {selectedSubjects.map((slug) => nameBySlug.get(slug) ?? slug).join(' + ')}
                      </span>
                    </>
                  )}
                  {q && (
                    <>
                      {' '}
                      matching <span class="text-base-content/80 not-italic">“{q}”</span>
                    </>
                  )}
                </p>
              )}

              {feed.items.length === 0 ? (
                <div class="flex min-h-[16rem] flex-col items-center justify-center px-6 py-16 text-center">
                  <p class="text-whisper max-w-sm font-serif text-lg">Nothing here yet.</p>
                  <p class="text-whisper mt-2 max-w-sm font-sans text-sm">
                    {selectedSubjects.length > 0 || q || authorSlug
                      ? `No ${nounPlural} match this filter.`
                      : `The ${nounPlural} will collect here.`}
                  </p>
                </div>
              ) : view === 'writing' ? (
                <div id="feed-list" class="divide-base-content/10 divide-y">
                  {writing!.items.map((item) => (
                    <PostCard item={item} term={q} />
                  ))}
                </div>
              ) : (
                <div id="feed-list" class="divide-base-content/10 divide-y">
                  {quotes!.items.map((item) => (
                    <QuoteCard item={item} term={q} />
                  ))}
                </div>
              )}

              {nextUrl && (
                <div id="feed-sentinel" data-next-url={nextUrl} class="feed-sentinel flex justify-center pt-10">
                  <a
                    href={nextUrl}
                    data-feed-more
                    class="feed-more text-whisper hover:text-base-content inline-flex items-center gap-1 font-sans text-sm transition-colors"
                  >
                    Load older {nounPlural}
                    <Icon name="ph:caret-down" class="size-3.5" />
                  </a>
                  <span class="feed-spin text-whisper font-sans text-sm">Loading…</span>
                </div>
              )}
            </div>
          </section>
        </div>
      )
    }
  </div>

  {
    /* The in-context reader. The writing view fills it from a `<template>` per
      card — the essay is already on the page, so opening one costs no fetch.
      See Reader.astro.

      ⚠ THE QUOTES VIEW USES IT TOO, as of 2026-08-10. Michael: *"when we click a
      quote, just how there's a reading viewer sheet for the writing on the blog,
      we should also have a sheet for the quote."* A quote card used to NAVIGATE
      to the permalink, which is the one place on the site where clicking a
      fragment threw the reader out of the list they were reading — a
      constellation's quote had opened a sheet since earlier the same day.

      The templates are cheap here in a way they are not for essays: a quote's
      whole apparatus is a few hundred bytes, where an essay's is ~51% of the
      document. */
  }
  <Reader returnLabel="Back to the blog" />
  {
    view === 'quotes' &&
      quotes!.items.map((q) => {
        const page = quoteSheets.get(q.id);
        return page ? (
          <template data-reader-content={q.slug}>
            <QuoteArticle page={page} />
          </template>
        ) : null;
      })
  }
</SiteLayout>

<script>
  // ⚠ `music-sets` USED TO BE IMPORTED HERE TOO and left with the room (ADR
  // 0040) — which is the one measurable win of the move: this route is the
  // busiest on the site, and it was shipping a Spotify controller to two views
  // that have never had a pane for it to take.
  import '../../scripts/blog-feed';
</script>
